**MOFCOM Clarification on the November 27 Rare-Earth Suspension Expiry**

In light of recent attention to the 27 November 2026 expiry of the rare-earth export-control suspension under MOFCOM Announcement No. 70 of 7 November 2025, the Government of the People's Republic of China wishes to clarify the legal and policy framework that will govern any decision on the post-expiry arrangement.

**The legal character of the suspension.** Announcement No. 70 was a sovereign act of restraint — a suspension, not a relinquishment. The underlying export-control authority over gallium, germanium, antimony, superhard materials, rare-earth-related items, and other dual-use goods is exercised under the Export Control Law of the People's Republic of China (2020) and the Dual-Use Items Export Control Regulations (2024). Those authorities remain in force. What expires on 27 November is the forbearance, not the jurisdiction.

**The principle of conditional reciprocity.** China's decision to suspend these measures in the Busan framework was an exercise of good faith made possible by the reciprocal restraint of the United States — including the absence, at the time of the Busan consensus, of further tightening of chip-export controls targeting the PRC. That condition has not been honored. Since 7 November 2025, the United States has:

- Tightened the BIS licensing regime and extended Approved IC Designer accommodations only as a bridge, not as a stable equilibrium;
- Closed the "ultimate parent headquartered in the PRC" loophole on 31 May 2026;
- Continued the layered application of Section 232 tariffs to semiconductors, steel and steel derivatives, and patented pharmaceuticals;
- Expanded Section 301 actions to 60 trading partners on the pretext of forced-labor findings.

Each of these actions has been undertaken without consultation and without regard to the spirit of the Busan consensus. Reciprocity, by definition, requires reciprocity.

**The forthcoming review.** MOFCOM will conduct a comprehensive review of the totality of US conduct across all relevant vectors — chip-export controls, Section 232 measures, Section 301 actions, and the broader climate of bilateral engagement — to determine whether the conditions for continued suspension are present. The review will be guided by the following considerations:

1. Whether US chip-export controls have stabilized, tightened, or expanded;
2. Whether Section 232 measures applied to conventional commercial goods have been narrowed, maintained, or expanded;
3. Whether Section 301 actions against China and third parties have been narrowed, maintained, or expanded;
4. Whether US rhetoric and policy posture support stable economic relations or signal further escalation.

The default outcome of this review, absent a material change in US conduct, is the orderly reactivation of China's export-control administration over the relevant items, consistent with the law and with WTO obligations. China reserves the right to take necessary measures to protect its sovereignty and development interests.

**To our partners.** The European Union, Japan, the Republic of Korea, and other economies that have engaged with China in good faith on critical-minerals supply stability are welcome to continue dialogue on licensing arrangements that reflect the spirit of mutual benefit and the realities of supply-chain interdependence. China does not seek to weaponize critical-minerals supply — but it will not forgo lawful regulatory authority while its legitimate development rights are constrained by unilateral measures.

**The path forward.** The choice before the United States is straightforward: honor the Busan framework in substance, and the conditions for continued suspension can be renewed; or persist in escalation, and accept the orderly reactivation of Chinese export controls in accordance with law. China has acted in good faith. It will continue to do so. But good faith must be met with good faith — and where it is not, China will defend its interests through lawful, proportionate, and transparent measures.

Further updates will be issued through MOFCOM in due course.
